# Settings row

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A settings row puts one setting on one line: a heading that names it, a short description of what it does and the control that changes it. Home Assistant uses it throughout its settings screens, dialogs and automation editor.

It is part of Home Assistant’s own screens. A dashboard author cannot place it in a card. This page is for people who read, review or extend those screens, and for anyone laying out a setting in the same way.

For how settings are grouped and saved, see [Settings and preferences](../patterns/settings.html). For read-only pairs of a name and a value, use [Summary list](summary-list.html).

This page covers:

- Settings row
  - `ha-settings-row`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

From left to right, or top to bottom on a narrow screen:

- An optional prefix, such as an icon.
- A heading that names the setting.
- An optional description under the heading, shown only when there is one.
- The control, in the default slot.

On a narrow screen the row stacks, with the control under the text and a thin line above the row. A row with a description is at least 72 pixels tall.

